<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chilean Civil Code</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1855">1855</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chilean Civil Code, drafted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andres-bello">Andrés Bello</a> and promulgated in December 1855, came into force in 1857. Drawing on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a>, Spanish legislation and the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-code">Napoleonic Code</a>, it regulated persons, property, contracts and inheritance in clear, systematic language. It was adopted almost unchanged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a> and influenced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a> of several other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin American</a> countries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chilean-civil-code.jpg" alt="Chilean Civil Code" width="288" height="466" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Codigo_Civil_Chile_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The code, also called the Code of Bello, was the result of several years of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andres-bello">Andrés Bello</a>'s own work, though it was officially presented as the work of several commissions of Congress. Bello delivered the complete draft on 22 November 1855, and President Manuel Montt sent it to Congress with a foreword by Bello. Congress passed it into law on 14 December 1855 and it came into force on 1 January 1857. It has been altered many times but has remained in force ever since.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>A preliminary title (articles 1 to 53) deals with law in general. Like the French code, it allows a law to be applied only once it has been duly promulgated and officially published, with allowances for delays in communication, so that no secret laws were possible; it forbids laws that apply to events before their passage and bars judges from issuing general rulings with the force of legislation.</p><p>Book I (articles 54 to 564) covers persons: birth and death, marriage and paternity, and legal persons. Book II (565 to 950) covers kinds of goods, ownership, possession and other rights in property; Book III (951 to 1436) succession, wills and donations; and Book IV (1437 to 2524) obligations and contracts, including torts. Books I and III were later heavily amended to end discrimination between children of married and unmarried parents. A final article set the date of entry into force and repealed earlier laws on the same matters.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The code is regarded as original in plan and content when set against the French code. It was adopted whole by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a> in 1858, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a> in 1859, Venezuela in 1862 (for that year only), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a> in 1867, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a> in 1880 (until 1899 and again from 1906), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a> in 1887 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a> after its separation from Colombia in 1903. Augusto Teixeira de Freitas, author of the Esboço de un Código Civil para Brasil, and Dalmacio Vélez Sársfield, the main author of the Argentine Civil Code, considered it the most important legal achievement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chilean Civil Code” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Andrés Bello, a Venezuelan scholar who settled in Chile, wrote its <b>Civil Code of 1855</b>, which was later adopted by Ecuador and Colombia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Republic of Chile · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
